WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:01.230
Lizzie Borden: The Unsolved Mystery

00:00:01.230 --> 00:00:06.450
On August 4th, 1892, Andrew and Abby Borden
were brutally murdered in their home in Fall

00:00:06.450 --> 00:00:08.050
River, Massachusetts.

00:00:08.050 --> 00:00:13.690
Their heads had been split open in a fit of
rage from nearly a dozen blows from a hatchet.

00:00:13.690 --> 00:00:16.590
There was no theft, and no sign of a break-in.

00:00:16.590 --> 00:00:20.950
Their 32-year old daughter, Lizzie Borden,
was at home when her parents were killed.

00:00:20.950 --> 00:00:25.270
She inherited her father’s multi-million
dollar fortune, and everyone in Fall River

00:00:25.270 --> 00:00:28.250
believed that she killed her parents in cold
blood.

00:00:28.250 --> 00:00:32.390
But in the end, there simply was not enough
evidence to convict her, and she was allowed

00:00:32.390 --> 00:00:33.650
to go free.

00:00:33.650 --> 00:00:37.680
Nearly 120 years later, the case is still
an unsolved mystery.

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:43.579
Did Lizzie Borden really kill her parents,
or is the identity of the real killer still

00:00:43.579 --> 00:00:45.430
unknown?

00:00:45.430 --> 00:00:58.510
The Borden Legacy, and Lizzie’s Childhood
Andrew Borden came from a long line of wealthy

00:00:58.510 --> 00:01:02.170
and successful people, but his own parents
struggled with poverty.

00:01:02.170 --> 00:01:07.180
As a young man, he started working as an underwriter
at a bank in Fall River, Massachusetts.

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:11.700
He used his paychecks to purchase investment
real estate, and ran furniture and textile

00:01:11.700 --> 00:01:12.700
businesses.

00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:15.680
He was eventually promoted as the manager
of the bank.

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:20.070
Even though he was making a lot of money,
he only paid for the bare necessities for

00:01:20.070 --> 00:01:22.100
himself and his family.

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:27.770
His fortune grew to over $300,000, which is
the modern-day equivalent of $8 million.

00:01:27.770 --> 00:01:32.060
He married a woman named Sarah Anthony Morse,
and they had three children together.

00:01:32.060 --> 00:01:33.060
Emma was the oldest.

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:36.310
They had a second daughter named Alice, who
died as a baby.

00:01:36.310 --> 00:01:40.130
Lizzie Borden was the youngest daughter, born
in 1860.

00:01:40.130 --> 00:01:43.850
When she was only two years old, Lizzie’s
mother Sarah became gravely ill.

00:01:43.850 --> 00:01:47.650
Emma was twelve at the time, so she was old
enough to remember the death of her sister

00:01:47.650 --> 00:01:51.290
Alice, and fully understood the gravity of
losing their mother.

00:01:51.290 --> 00:01:55.430
On her deathbed, Emma swore to take care of
baby Lizzie like a mother, and she took that

00:01:55.430 --> 00:01:57.750
promise very seriously.

00:01:57.750 --> 00:02:01.800
Three years after Sarah’s death, Andrew
Borden married a woman named Abby Durfee.

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:06.680
Emma, who was 15 at the time, refused to address
Abby her as “mother”, and called her by

00:02:06.680 --> 00:02:08.929
her first name their entire childhood.

00:02:08.929 --> 00:02:12.870
Lizzie grew up to be very close with her sister
Emma, who truly did feel more like a mother

00:02:12.870 --> 00:02:14.239
than a big sister.

00:02:14.239 --> 00:02:18.730
However, this backfired, because teenage Emma
was constantly reminding her younger sister

00:02:18.730 --> 00:02:20.900
that their birth mother was dead.

00:02:20.900 --> 00:02:24.540
Because of this, Lizzie never formed a close
relationship with her stepmother.

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:28.740
She later described their relationship as
cordial, but she grew up feeling as though

00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:30.580
she never really knew a mother’s love.

00:02:30.580 --> 00:02:34.760
We don’t know much about the intimate details
of Lizzie’s relationship with her father,

00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:39.459
except that he was very busy with his full-time
job at the bank, and running his businesses

00:02:39.459 --> 00:02:40.459
on the side.

00:02:40.459 --> 00:02:44.570
Lizzie claimed to love him very much, but
his shrewd business tactics and lack of empathy

00:02:44.570 --> 00:02:50.280
for customers defaulting on their loans caused
some people in town to dislike him and his

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:51.280
family.

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:55.550
Lizzie Borden’s elementary school teachers
described her as being aloof, so she was either

00:02:55.550 --> 00:02:58.540
painfully shy, or felt as though she didn’t
want to make friends.

00:02:58.540 --> 00:03:01.220
She was also known for having dramatic mood
swings.

00:03:01.220 --> 00:03:07.400
She could either be very relaxed and unemotional,
or she could burst out in fits of rage.

00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:10.790
This is not uncommon for children who have
lost their parents at a young age, but she

00:03:10.790 --> 00:03:19.569
was severely neglected, considering the tragedy
that had befallen her family.

00:03:19.569 --> 00:03:23.600
Adulthood
The wealthy members of the extended Borden

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:27.989
family lived in mansions up in a fashionable
neighborhood called The Hill.

00:03:27.989 --> 00:03:32.379
They enjoyed an easy life with teams of servants
at their beckon call.

00:03:32.379 --> 00:03:36.879
Andrew Borden, on the other hand, rented half
of a duplex for his family, and waited until

00:03:36.879 --> 00:03:41.810
his children were grown up to buy that house
and combine both halves into one single home.

00:03:41.810 --> 00:03:46.820
He never installed electricity or indoor plumbing,
even though it was actually quite affordable

00:03:46.820 --> 00:03:47.820
at that time.

00:03:47.820 --> 00:03:51.430
As she grew older, Lizzie began to learn more
about her wealthy extended family who lived

00:03:51.430 --> 00:03:52.470
up on The Hill.

00:03:52.470 --> 00:03:56.620
Her cousins were always attending parties
and dances, and went to the local theater

00:03:56.620 --> 00:03:58.890
together with young people their own age.

00:03:58.890 --> 00:04:02.673
Lizzie wanted to join the rest of her friends,
but Andrew Borden thought that all of these

00:04:02.673 --> 00:04:05.390
activities were a waste of money.

00:04:05.390 --> 00:04:10.360
He never gave his daughters an allowance to
attend any of the social outings.

00:04:10.360 --> 00:04:14.340
Living in a working-class neighborhood, Emma
and Lizzie seemed far too rich to fit in where

00:04:14.340 --> 00:04:18.329
they lived, and yet their father never gave
them money to socialize on The Hill.

00:04:18.329 --> 00:04:22.430
They were stuck in an isolating situation,
and felt like outcasts.

00:04:22.430 --> 00:04:26.159
Her cousins all married rich men, and their
fathers paid dowries to help them buy their

00:04:26.159 --> 00:04:27.159
own houses.

00:04:27.159 --> 00:04:31.339
Meanwhile, Lizzie and Emma were still living
with their parents, and their only social

00:04:31.339 --> 00:04:34.039
interactions came from church-related events.

00:04:34.039 --> 00:04:39.889
Lizzie Borden really only had one close friend;
her neighbor, Alice Russell.

00:04:39.889 --> 00:04:43.699
Many people who knew Lizzie Borden before
the murders claimed that she was a gentle

00:04:43.699 --> 00:04:45.779
person with a very kind heart.

00:04:45.779 --> 00:04:50.180
She would leave slices of chocolate cake for
men who came to her father’s house for maintenance,

00:04:50.180 --> 00:04:54.540
and insisted on tipping the groundskeeper
whenever her father was too cheap to do it

00:04:54.540 --> 00:04:55.540
himself.

00:04:55.540 --> 00:05:00.559
She also gave candy to the local children,
along with cards signed “From Aunty Borden”.

00:05:00.559 --> 00:05:05.620
Even though women were not allowed to hold
a job at that time, Lizzie kept herself busy.

00:05:05.620 --> 00:05:09.680
She taught Sunday School to the local children
in Fall River, and most people in the town

00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:13.729
knew of her as being a very pious and religious
woman.

00:05:13.729 --> 00:05:16.800
She was also heavily involved in local women’s
charity groups.

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:21.080
While she testified that she loved her father
very much, Lizzie fundamentally disagreed

00:05:21.080 --> 00:05:23.300
with him in the way he handled his money.

00:05:23.300 --> 00:05:27.020
She also believed that her stepmother was
trying to take advantage of his fortune.

00:05:27.020 --> 00:05:31.539
When she was 27 years old, Lizzie’s father
gave a house to his wife’s half-sister for

00:05:31.539 --> 00:05:32.539
free.

00:05:32.539 --> 00:05:36.879
This made Lizzie and Emma furious, because
they were his own daughters, and yet he had

00:05:36.879 --> 00:05:39.199
never given them such an extravagant gift.

00:05:39.199 --> 00:05:43.249
When he realized how unfair this was, he gave
his daughters the deed to the house he grew

00:05:43.249 --> 00:05:45.219
up in to try to pacify them.

00:05:45.219 --> 00:05:50.279
However, there was a strict code of etiquette
that unmarried women could not live alone.

00:05:50.279 --> 00:05:53.869
Since Emma and Lizzie never found husbands,
owning the deed to their grandfather’s home

00:05:53.869 --> 00:05:58.719
was virtually useless, and they were never
allowed to move out of their father’s house.

00:05:58.719 --> 00:06:02.030
They eventually sold the house back to their
father for $5,000.

00:06:02.030 --> 00:06:07.039
Even after doing this, it was still not enough
to make up for the jealousy and resentment

00:06:07.039 --> 00:06:08.520
Lizzie felt towards her stepmother Abby.

00:06:08.520 --> 00:06:09.520
She stopped calling her “mother”, and
only referred to her as “Mrs. Borden”.

00:06:09.520 --> 00:06:13.839
Lizzie Borden was unhappy living with her
father and stepmother, and she spent as much

00:06:13.839 --> 00:06:16.199
time out of the house as she possibly could.

00:06:16.199 --> 00:06:20.249
Soon after their argument about the houses,
Andrew Borden agreed to let Lizzie visit Europe,

00:06:20.249 --> 00:06:24.729
but the trip abroad only made her realize
just how much she had missed in her younger

00:06:24.729 --> 00:06:25.729
years.

00:06:25.729 --> 00:06:28.639
When she returned home to the United States,
she often chose not to eat meals with her

00:06:28.639 --> 00:06:32.369
parents, and went up to her bedroom without
saying hello.

00:06:32.369 --> 00:06:36.059
Life in the Borden house went on like this
for several more years.

00:06:36.059 --> 00:06:41.119
In 1892, Lizzie Borden was 32 years old, and
her sister Emma was 42.

00:06:41.119 --> 00:06:42.939
Neither of them had gotten married.

00:06:42.939 --> 00:06:47.559
In the 1800’s, this made them “spinsters”,
or women who were considered to be far too

00:06:47.559 --> 00:06:49.159
old to find a husband.

00:06:49.159 --> 00:06:53.629
There is no record of why they were never
married, but their family issues clearly had

00:06:53.629 --> 00:06:54.849
something to do with it.

00:06:54.849 --> 00:06:59.529
Lizzie continued to devote herself to church,
and the local animal rescue.

00:06:59.529 --> 00:07:04.240
She was raising a family of pigeons on their
property, and she loved them like pets.

00:07:04.240 --> 00:07:08.620
In June of that same year, Andrew purchased
a hatchet, and started killing Lizzie’s

00:07:08.620 --> 00:07:11.319
birds so he could save money on meat.

00:07:11.319 --> 00:07:15.340
Lizzie may have felt that that her window
of opportunity to escape this dysfunctional

00:07:15.340 --> 00:07:18.099
family and find a better life was closing
in on her.

00:07:18.099 --> 00:07:22.580
Her father was 70 years old, and she needed
to face the possibility that he may die soon.

00:07:22.580 --> 00:07:28.000
If his money was controlled by her stepmother
Abby, she knew things would get much worse.

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:32.830
At any moment, Lizzie Borden and her sister
Emma’s lives were going to be over...Unless

00:07:32.830 --> 00:07:35.860
they did something to stop it.

00:07:35.860 --> 00:07:43.889
The Day Of The Murders
As we mentioned earlier, Andrew and Abby Borden

00:07:43.889 --> 00:07:46.949
were murdered on August 4th, 1892.

00:07:46.949 --> 00:07:51.770
The events leading up to their murders may
give a clue as to what may have really happened

00:07:51.770 --> 00:07:53.129
on that fateful day.

00:07:53.129 --> 00:07:56.599
On August 2nd, the entire Borden family was
feeling sick.

00:07:56.599 --> 00:08:01.569
Abby Borden visited her local doctor, asking
if it was possible that her bread had been

00:08:01.569 --> 00:08:02.569
poisoned.

00:08:02.569 --> 00:08:06.089
Her doctor said that it was far more likely
that she simply ate some rotten meat, and

00:08:06.089 --> 00:08:08.999
that everyone in the house was suffering from
food poisoning.

00:08:08.999 --> 00:08:14.300
This not uncommon back then, since there were
no refrigerators to keep meat and cheese from

00:08:14.300 --> 00:08:16.580
going bad in the summer heat.

00:08:16.580 --> 00:08:21.080
On August 3rd, the night before the murders,
Lizzie and Emma’s uncle John Morse showed

00:08:21.080 --> 00:08:22.520
up unannounced.

00:08:22.520 --> 00:08:25.830
He did not bring any luggage, but he asked
to stay the night.

00:08:25.830 --> 00:08:30.669
John Morse was the brother of Sarah Morse,
Andrew’s first wife, and the girl’s biological

00:08:30.669 --> 00:08:31.669
uncle.

00:08:31.669 --> 00:08:34.529
For whatever reason, this made Lizzie Borden
feel uneasy.

00:08:34.529 --> 00:08:38.740
She told her friend, Alice Russell, that she
was terrified that someone was about to hurt

00:08:38.740 --> 00:08:39.740
their family.

00:08:39.740 --> 00:08:45.069
According to his testimony, John Morse woke
up at 6AM the next day, on August 4th.

00:08:45.069 --> 00:08:49.879
Bridget, the maid, cooked food for her masters
and guest, and served their breakfast to Abby,

00:08:49.879 --> 00:08:52.410
Andrew, and John around 7AM.

00:08:52.410 --> 00:08:56.459
John announced that he was going to see his
other relatives in town, and left the house

00:08:56.459 --> 00:08:58.760
around 8:30 in the morning.

00:08:58.760 --> 00:09:02.529
Lizzie said that she was feeling sick, so
she never said hello to her uncle, or came

00:09:02.529 --> 00:09:04.529
down to eat breakfast with her family.

00:09:04.529 --> 00:09:07.300
Andrew Borden went to work at the bank around
9AM.

00:09:07.300 --> 00:09:11.769
According to the maid, Bridget, Mrs. Borden
asked her to wash the windows outside that

00:09:11.769 --> 00:09:16.130
day, and she said that she would make the
bed and do the laundry in the guest bedroom.

00:09:16.130 --> 00:09:19.930
When Bridget was done washing the windows,
she was feeling exhausted from working in

00:09:19.930 --> 00:09:23.070
the summer heat, and she was still sick to
her stomach.

00:09:23.070 --> 00:09:26.899
She had been vomiting in the backyard, and
desperately needed to rest.

00:09:26.899 --> 00:09:31.459
Andrew was feeling sick at work, so he returned
home around 11AM, but all of the doors were

00:09:31.459 --> 00:09:35.370
locked with a dead bolt, so his key to the
front door would not work.

00:09:35.370 --> 00:09:38.740
This was strange, and he pounded on the door
to have someone let him in.

00:09:38.740 --> 00:09:41.100
Bridget rushed to the front door to unlock
it.

00:09:41.100 --> 00:09:44.980
Andrew Borden decided to take a nap on the
living room couch, hoping he could sleep off

00:09:44.980 --> 00:09:46.930
his sickness during his lunch break.

00:09:46.930 --> 00:09:50.940
Bridget saw this as an opportunity to rest,
so she went up to her bedroom in the attic

00:09:50.940 --> 00:09:52.240
to take a nap as well.

00:09:52.240 --> 00:09:56.930
Lizzie claims that she had to go outside to
the barn to look for fishing lures for a trip

00:09:56.930 --> 00:09:58.750
she was taking the following weekend.

00:09:58.750 --> 00:10:03.080
According to her testimony, during that small
10 to 15-minute window of time outside, someone

00:10:03.080 --> 00:10:06.760
had taken a hatchet to her father’s face
11 times.

00:10:06.760 --> 00:10:11.399
His eyeballs were cut in half, his nose was
gone, and he was barely recognizable as a

00:10:11.399 --> 00:10:12.399
person anymore.

00:10:12.399 --> 00:10:16.519
Lizzie walked back into the house, and when
she saw her father’s mutilated body.

00:10:16.519 --> 00:10:20.410
She screamed, and called out to Bridget that
her father was dead.

00:10:20.410 --> 00:10:24.900
She ran next door to a neighbor named Adelaide
Churchill, saying, “Oh Mrs. Churchill, do

00:10:24.900 --> 00:10:25.930
come over.

00:10:25.930 --> 00:10:27.040
Someone has killed father.”

00:10:27.040 --> 00:10:31.360
They notified the police and the family doctor,
who arrived to examine the body.

00:10:31.360 --> 00:10:34.990
By that time, everyone in the neighborhood
knew about the crime.

00:10:34.990 --> 00:10:39.490
Andrew Borden’s wounds were still fresh,
suggesting that the attack happened very recently.

00:10:39.490 --> 00:10:43.370
Lizzie’s friend, Alice Russell, rushed over
to the house to see what was wrong.

00:10:43.370 --> 00:10:46.750
Lizzie was hysterical, and leaned on Alice
for comfort.

00:10:46.750 --> 00:10:50.870
Lizzie asked Bridget to check upstairs for
her stepmother, saying, “Will someone find

00:10:50.870 --> 00:10:52.029
Mrs. Borden?”

00:10:52.029 --> 00:10:56.029
Mrs. Borden was lying face-down on the floor
of one of the guest bedrooms.

00:10:56.029 --> 00:11:00.040
She had also been killed by an ax, but this
time, it was in the back of the head.

00:11:00.040 --> 00:11:04.029
Mrs. Borden’s blood was dry, and the medical
examiners estimated that she died roughly

00:11:04.029 --> 00:11:05.959
two hours before her husband.

00:11:05.959 --> 00:11:10.220
This would mean that the killer must have
been in the house the entire time, waiting

00:11:10.220 --> 00:11:12.660
for the perfect opportunity to kill Andrew
Borden.

00:11:12.660 --> 00:11:17.089
Lizzie’s sister Emma was out of town visiting
a friend, and they had to send a telegram,

00:11:17.089 --> 00:11:18.470
telling her to come home.

00:11:18.470 --> 00:11:23.060
She had a solid alibi about her whereabouts,
so the police removed her as potential suspect.

00:11:23.060 --> 00:11:27.089
Bridget the maid was home, but she had nothing
to gain by killing her masters.

00:11:27.089 --> 00:11:30.509
She was also removed as a potential suspect
by the police.

00:11:30.509 --> 00:11:33.980
When the police arrived, it was noted that
no one in the house was covered in blood.

00:11:33.980 --> 00:11:38.220
If Lizzie Borden really did kill her father,
she would have had blood splattered all over

00:11:38.220 --> 00:11:39.220
her clothes.

00:11:39.220 --> 00:11:42.660
She would not have been able to clean herself
off within a matter of 10 minutes, especially

00:11:42.660 --> 00:11:44.610
without running water in the house.

00:11:44.610 --> 00:11:48.540
But, considering that she was one of the only
people home at the time, and she had the most

00:11:48.540 --> 00:11:52.759
to gain from her parents’ deaths, all eyes
were on her.

00:11:52.759 --> 00:12:00.999
The Inquest and Trial
Lizzie and Emma Borden stood with their family

00:12:00.999 --> 00:12:05.009
members and her father’s business associates
at their parent’s funeral.

00:12:05.009 --> 00:12:08.459
Instead of watching their parents’ caskets
get buried in the ground, the police ordered

00:12:08.459 --> 00:12:10.980
the bodies to be taken for a second autopsy.

00:12:10.980 --> 00:12:15.220
The police also had free reign to search the
entire house from top to bottom, and yet they

00:12:15.220 --> 00:12:19.850
could not find a murder weapon, or any solid
evidence that would prove the identity of

00:12:19.850 --> 00:12:20.850
the killer.

00:12:20.850 --> 00:12:24.620
Lizzie Borden was questioned for several hours
by the police, and her answers were sometimes

00:12:24.620 --> 00:12:28.300
inconsistent, as if she couldn’t get her
story straight.

00:12:28.300 --> 00:12:31.120
The police saw this as evidence that she was
lying.

00:12:31.120 --> 00:12:35.420
However, her doctor had injected her with
morphine before each interview, saying that

00:12:35.420 --> 00:12:37.319
it would help calm her nerves.

00:12:37.319 --> 00:12:41.180
These injections would have made her feel
mentally cloudy and confused.

00:12:41.180 --> 00:12:45.329
In some cases, the drug can even cause delusions
and hallucinations.

00:12:45.329 --> 00:12:49.750
Despite having this drug running through her
veins, she still never confessed to killing

00:12:49.750 --> 00:12:51.100
her parents.

00:12:51.100 --> 00:12:54.720
Leading up to the trial, Emma Borden and Alice
Russell witnessed Lizzie Borden burning one

00:12:54.720 --> 00:12:56.560
of her dresses in the backyard.

00:12:56.560 --> 00:13:00.740
She claimed that she was simply disposing
of old clothes that had paint stains on them.

00:13:00.740 --> 00:13:05.449
She gave the police the blue dress that everyone
had witnessed her wearing the day of the murders,

00:13:05.449 --> 00:13:07.699
and it was completely free of blood.

00:13:07.699 --> 00:13:10.870
They also never found a bloody dress when
searching the house.

00:13:10.870 --> 00:13:15.060
But the police saw the burning of the mystery
dress as a sign that she may have changed

00:13:15.060 --> 00:13:19.709
out of the clothes she killed her parents
in, and she was trying to destroy the evidence.

00:13:19.709 --> 00:13:23.470
Lizzie Borden was arrested on suspicion of
killing her parents, and she was taken to

00:13:23.470 --> 00:13:25.230
a holding cell in a women’s prison.

00:13:25.230 --> 00:13:31.189
The day of the trial was from June 5th to
the 20th of 1893, so it was nearly a year

00:13:31.189 --> 00:13:32.769
after the death of her parents.

00:13:32.769 --> 00:13:37.120
A pharmacist named Eli Bence testified in
court that Lizzie Borden requested to buy

00:13:37.120 --> 00:13:41.301
potassium cyanide the day before the murder,
but he refused to sell it to her, because

00:13:41.301 --> 00:13:43.899
he suspected that she had terrible intentions.

00:13:43.899 --> 00:13:47.730
When the police exhumed the bodies of Mr.
and Mrs. Borden, the autopsy report showed

00:13:47.730 --> 00:13:49.879
that they had ever been poisoned.

00:13:49.879 --> 00:13:51.930
It was just bad food, after all.

00:13:51.930 --> 00:13:55.810
So Mr. Bence’s claim didn’t prove that
Lizzie did anything wrong.

00:13:55.810 --> 00:14:00.130
Her neighbor and friend, Alice Russell, also
testified against her about burning her dress.

00:14:00.130 --> 00:14:04.519
However, without having the actual dress on-hand,
they could not prove that there was ever blood

00:14:04.519 --> 00:14:05.519
on it.

00:14:05.519 --> 00:14:10.180
Even after having a year to prepare their
case, the police only had circumstantial evidence

00:14:10.180 --> 00:14:14.279
against Lizzie Borden, and it was simply not
enough to prove that she was guilty.

00:14:14.279 --> 00:14:17.600
The members of the jury were all men, which
was customary of the time.

00:14:17.600 --> 00:14:21.459
All of them had a very hard time believing
that the same woman who was the Sunday school

00:14:21.459 --> 00:14:26.490
teacher could really be the same monster who
committed cold-blooded murder.

00:14:26.490 --> 00:14:30.449
They thought it was more likely that a prowler
broke into the home and killed Mr. and Mrs.

00:14:30.449 --> 00:14:31.449
Borden.

00:14:31.449 --> 00:14:36.300
They only took 90 minutes to make the decision
that she was not guilty.

00:14:36.300 --> 00:14:43.740
Lizzie Borden Goes Free
After her parent’s deaths, Lizzie and her

00:14:43.740 --> 00:14:47.690
sister Emma split their father’s inheritance
in half, and it was more than enough money

00:14:47.690 --> 00:14:50.129
to take care of them for the rest of their
lives.

00:14:50.129 --> 00:14:54.329
They could finally afford to buy a luxurious
house on The Hill called Maplecroft.

00:14:54.329 --> 00:14:58.450
They also hired a staff of maids and a driver
to take them anywhere they wanted to go in

00:14:58.450 --> 00:14:59.930
a horse-drawn coach.

00:14:59.930 --> 00:15:04.329
Lizzie changed her name to “Lizbeth” after
moving, in hopes of escaping her reputation.

00:15:04.329 --> 00:15:09.610
In her mind, the life on The Hill may have
seemed like a million miles away, but the

00:15:09.610 --> 00:15:12.480
reality was that they were still in Fall River.

00:15:12.480 --> 00:15:16.870
Her reputation as a murderer continued to
follow her for the rest of her life.

00:15:16.870 --> 00:15:21.249
Magazines reported false rumors that she was
a shoplifter, and that she killed baby kittens

00:15:21.249 --> 00:15:23.770
and birds as a child.

00:15:23.770 --> 00:15:27.939
People did not want to sit near her in church,
and her house was pummelled with rotten eggs

00:15:27.939 --> 00:15:29.439
on a regular basis.

00:15:29.439 --> 00:15:34.199
No one wanted to be associated with this woman
who may have murdered her parents.

00:15:34.199 --> 00:15:39.389
Local children made up a rhyme about her murder,
and enjoyed singing it outside her window:

00:15:39.389 --> 00:15:41.110
“Lizzie Borden took an axe.

00:15:41.110 --> 00:15:42.490
She gave her mother forty whacks.

00:15:42.490 --> 00:15:47.040
When she saw what she had done, she gave her
father forty-one.”

00:15:47.040 --> 00:15:51.600
Despite all of the criticism, Lizzie never
moved far away to escape the ridicule, and

00:15:51.600 --> 00:15:53.519
tried to make the best of her situation.

00:15:53.519 --> 00:15:57.399
She began attending plays at the local theater,
and threw parties in her new home for the

00:15:57.399 --> 00:15:58.470
cast and crew.

00:15:58.470 --> 00:16:02.550
She seemed to be particularly infatuated with
an actress named Nance O’Neil.

00:16:02.550 --> 00:16:06.410
O’Neil was known for having a deep voice
and a masculine attitude, which lead some

00:16:06.410 --> 00:16:09.290
people to believe that two may have been having
an affair.

00:16:09.290 --> 00:16:13.650
It turns out that O’Neil truly was a great
actress worthy of admiration, though, because

00:16:13.650 --> 00:16:17.170
she eventually moved to Hollywood and starred
in a few movies.

00:16:17.170 --> 00:16:21.480
Even though Lizzie Borden was now wealthy
and living in a good part of town, she continued

00:16:21.480 --> 00:16:26.370
to be single for the rest of her life, but
she may have been concealing a forbidden romance.

00:16:26.370 --> 00:16:31.220
The only real evidence of this was a letter
she wrote to a female friend, saying something

00:16:31.220 --> 00:16:36.369
rather romantic; “I dreamed of you the other
night, but I dare not put my dreams on paper.”

00:16:36.369 --> 00:16:40.889
For some unknown reason, Emma and Lizzie got
into an argument, and Emma decided it was

00:16:40.889 --> 00:16:42.940
time for her to move out of Fall River.

00:16:42.940 --> 00:16:47.870
She moved far away to escape the bad reputation
that the Borden name brought them, and the

00:16:47.870 --> 00:16:49.990
sisters never saw each other again.

00:16:49.990 --> 00:16:53.920
Some people speculate that this is proof of
Lizzie’s guilt, and that she must have confessed

00:16:53.920 --> 00:16:54.920
to Emma.

00:16:54.920 --> 00:16:58.980
However, Emma left shortly after one of Lizzie’s
big parties, in which the actress Nance O'Neill

00:16:58.980 --> 00:17:00.189
had attended.

00:17:00.189 --> 00:17:05.409
Some people believe that it may be more likely
that she was caught in a lesbian affair, or

00:17:05.409 --> 00:17:08.399
Emma simply disapproved of her new lifestyle.

00:17:08.399 --> 00:17:12.860
Lizzie Borden continued to live alone in her
Maplecroft mansion for the rest of her life.

00:17:12.860 --> 00:17:14.669
She died at the age of 66.

00:17:14.669 --> 00:17:19.890
She left a significant portion of her estate
to the Fall River Animal Rescue League in

00:17:19.890 --> 00:17:20.890
her will.

00:17:20.890 --> 00:17:24.130
She also left thousands of dollars to each
of her close friends and cousins.

00:17:24.130 --> 00:17:28.890
Today, both her childhood home by the river
and Maplecroft mansion were purchased by an

00:17:28.890 --> 00:17:31.669
investor and turned into museums.

00:17:31.669 --> 00:17:41.830
To this day, people still speculate whether
Lizzie Borden truly

00:17:41.830 --> 00:17:57.210
did kill her parents, but the world may never
know.

